:translate-x-1 group-hover:-translate-y-1 transition-transform","children":[["$","path","1ffxy3",{"d":"M14.536 21.686a.5.5 0 0 0 .937-.024l6.5-19a.496.496 0 0 0-.635-.635l-19 6.5a.5.5 0 0 0-.024.937l7.93 3.18a2 2 0 0 1 1.112 1.11z"}],["$","path","12cjpa",{"d":"m21.854 2.147-10.94 10.939"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl border border-primary/10","children":["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm italic text-muted-foreground text-center","children":"\"Each journey is carefully crafted to ensure you experience the soul of India.\""}]}]]}]}]
c:T78e,<p>Mysore is to the South what Jaipur is to the North. It is a city of splendid palaces, fragrant gardens, fine craftsmanship and enduring traditions. Yet beyond its celebrated royal heritage lies a quieter, more intimate side of the region—one that reveals itself through village life, ancient temples, silk production and the extraordinary natural world of the Cauvery River.<br><br>A heritage walk through Mysore lets you discover the stories behind its grand avenues, historic buildings, colourful markets and palaces. At Ranganathittu Bird Sanctuary, take to the waterways by boat or explore its tranquil surroundings in search of painted storks, pelicans, herons, egrets and other wetland species. The sanctuary's small islands and wooded banks provide a wonderful setting for observing birdlife at close quarters, particularly in the early morning when the river is at its most peaceful.<br><br>Nearby, Somnathpur offers one of southern India's great architectural treasures: the exquisite Hoysala temple dedicated to Lord Keshava. Built in the 13th century, its intricately carved stonework is extraordinary in both detail and delicacy. A visit here is an opportunity to appreciate the artistry of the Hoysala craftsmen, whose work captures scenes from mythology, music, dance and everyday life in an astonishingly intricate stone narrative.<br><br>Venture beyond the city to meet silk farmers and learn about the remarkable journey from the humble silkworm cocoon to the luxurious fabric for which Mysore has long been renowned. Meeting those involved in this traditional craft offers a rare glimpse into rural life and the skill, patience and knowledge behind India's finest textiles.<br><br>Royal heritage, extraordinary craftsmanship, ancient architecture, wildlife and encounters with local communities combine to create a richly layered experience of southern India—cultured, and an authentic experience.<br><br></p>d:T420,<p>Known as the “Scotland of India”, Coorg is a captivating corner of Karnataka where misty hills, dense forests and coffee plantations create an atmosphere of quiet seclusion. The landscape is beautifully green, particularly during the monsoon, with winding roads passing through estates scented with coffee, cardamom and pepper.<br>Coorg is also home to the Kodava people, a distinctive community with their own traditions, cuisine and fascinating history. A visit to a coffee estate offers an opportunity to discover the journey from blossom to bean, meet growers and enjoy the remarkable hospitality for which the region is known. Elsewhere, explore forest trails, seek out birdlife and wildlife, or simply pause to enjoy the views from a beautifully situated plantation retreat.<br>For the discerning traveller, Coorg is less about a list of sights and more about time spent in the landscape — lingering over freshly brewed coffee, walking beneath towering trees and experiencing the gentle rhythm of life in Karnataka’s highlands.<br><br></p>6:["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-7xl mx-auto","children":["$","$Lb",null,{"places":[{"id":14,"title":"Hyderabad","slug":"hyderabad","description":"<p>Hyderabad, the capital of Telangana, is a captivating city where of history and culture blend with modern urban life. Known as the “City of Pearls”, Hyderabad is famous for its magnificent landmarks, including the iconic Charminar, Golconda Fort, Chowmahalla Palace and Qutb Shahi Tombs, which reflect the city’s rich Indo-Islamic and royal heritage. Some of the main sights include the Salar Jung Museum, Falaknuma Palace and lively traditional markets such as Laad Bazaar. Hyderabad is equally celebrated for its distinctive cuisine, particularly Hyderabadi biryani and Irani chai.Hyderabad is very spread out but does have areas that one can walk around. It is a great place to walk around and experience life, culture and architecture of this truly amazing city.<br><br></p>","image":"https://captivationgindia.bvox.in/storage/places/zCqKM1sEz78tqQpuQDRX4h7iUz4Fz3B8wqjdXjES.jpg","activities_count":0},{"id":15,"title":"Mysore","slug":"mysore","description":"$c","image":"https://captivationgindia.bvox.in/storage/places/kKVcmaugEVpWISWTcds07Qk0wGO1I3XrUeFrnUUT.jpg","activities_count":0},{"id":17,"title":"Coorg","slug":"coorg","description":"$d","image":"https://captivationgindia.bvox.in/storage/places/DwBbDKwJaJkVfjtFj3x5nUkc2j6fGGzEyMtFguwM.jpg","activities_count":0},{"id":19,"title":"Hampi","slug":"hampi","description":"<p>Hampi is a remarkable landscape of ancient temples, sculpted boulders and ruined palaces, scattered across the banks of the Tungabhadra River. Once the magnificent capital of the Vijayanagara Empire, its monumental remains sit beautifully within the surrounding countryside. Explore the temples and bazaars with a knowledgeable local guide, discover quieter corners away from the main monuments, and experience the timeless atmosphere of this extraordinary UNESCO World Heritage Site.</p>","image":"https://captivationgindia.bvox.in/storage/places/CtkllH4DnR6SZDLRmwVdkvzI8Pe2n1HXGs8gb7jM.jpg","activities_count":0}],"destinationTitle":"South India"}]}]
